winebus: the vibration preference reaches a pad on a cable too.

mgvf-0009 gave a DualSense two per-device values -- VibrationMode and
VibrationGain -- that rewrite what a game asks of the motors. It was written
from a Bluetooth trace and came out Bluetooth-shaped in two places that have
nothing to do with the preference itself: the common block starts at byte 3 of
a 0x31, and the packet is signed. So its rewrite refuses anything that is not a
78-byte 0x31, and dualsense_vibration_options refused to read the values at all
for a pad that arrived on USB.

THE DEFECT IS MEASURED, and better than by a sentence. emulation-111440.log
holds both transports in one session under one registry:

  :11767  bus_create_hid_device desc {vid 054c, pid 0df2, ... bus_type 2}
  :11765  dualsense_vibration_options DualSense 054c:0df2 on Bluetooth:
          output reports will be rewritten -- the haptic path becomes the
          legacy compatible motors, gain 100%

  :20179  bus_create_hid_device desc {vid 054c, pid 0df2, ... bus_type 1}
          (and no dualsense_vibration_options line at all)

Same pad, same preference, eighty seconds apart. One transport reads the values
and the other silently does not. That is what the owner reported on 2026-09-09:
"con usb no toma los niveles de vibracion".

WHAT THE WIRE DIFFERS BY, and both halves of it are measured rather than
assumed. A wired pad is written report 0x02, whose common block starts at byte
1 -- which is what dualsense_bt_output_from_usb has been doing since mgvf-0005,
memcpy(out + 3, in + 1, ...) -- and which carries NO CRC: across 10,684 captured
0x02 writes not one has a tail equal to crc32(seed 0xa2) over its own bytes, and
10,683 of them end in four zero bytes. The one that does not, a 48-byte write
ending 24 00 00 40, is disproof by itself: ten milliseconds earlier the same
title wrote an id followed by 47 zeros, and no CRC over that payload is zero.
Those four bytes are ordinary report bytes.

So this adds a second ENVELOPE and not a second rewrite. Both entry points stand
on the one static function underneath, and the host test asserts that the same
common block through both comes out identical. The lengths come from the pads'
own descriptors -- 47 data bytes on a DualSense, 63 on an Edge -- so the flag
byte at common 38 sits inside both; a report too short to carry it is refused
rather than read past its end.

NEITHER ENTRY POINT IS CHOSEN BY A BRANCH, and that is the shape of the patch
rather than a detail. The obvious way to write it is to pick by the report id,
and the id to hand is the CLIENT's -- which on mgvf-0005's route is 0x02 over a
buffer already packed into a 78-byte 0x31. That was written here first, and it
sent Bluetooth packets to the wired rewrite, which refused them for their
length: mgvf-0009 stopped working on BOTH of its own routes, silently and
safely. Each entry point already examines report[0] and the length for itself
and touches nothing when it declines, so the buffer is offered to both in order
and there is no branch left to get wrong. The TRACE prints report[0] for the
same reason: on that route the client's id names a report the pad will never
see, and this line is the instrument the hand tests are read with.

WHAT IS ESTABLISHED IS THE DEFECT, NOT THE BENEFIT, and saying so is the point
of this paragraph. The Bluetooth case behind mgvf-0009 is a corpus: 6493 output
reports, 335 carrying a motor, all 335 on the haptic path. The cable has a
corpus too and it says the opposite -- of those same 10,684 wired writes, NOT
ONE carries a non-zero motor byte. Byte 39, the haptic path's own bit, is 0x00
in every one; six set the legacy bit and ask for zero with it. On everything
captured so far this patch is a NO-OP: with no motor to scale the gain leaves
the packet alone, and with no haptic bit the mode rewrite finds nothing to move.
What a wired title would ask of the motors if it asked at all is UNKNOWN.

AND THE REPORT THAT PROMPTED THIS MAY NOT BE ABOUT IT. A wired pad driven
through mgvf-0010's haptics collection already honours VibrationGain -- that
value is read into ext->haptics_gain for both transports and the haptics thread
applies it on the cable itself. What this closes is narrower than the sentence
that prompted it: a client writing the pad's OWN 0x02 with a motor in it, which
the captures show wired titles never doing. Whether the title in the owner's
hands is one of those is not established. The first use of this belongs in a
+hid trace, where dualsense_vibration_fixup's own line says what it changed.

mgvf-0010's haptics thread is deliberately left alone. It never passes the
dispatch this lives in, so there is nothing to apply twice, and it must not be
routed through here: it works on a report of five bytes where this one insists
on forty. The arithmetic in the two is the same three lines.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
