+/* MGVF mgvf-0016: the same preference, on a cable.
+ *
+ * WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. mgvf-0009 was written for a pad on Bluetooth and is
+ * shaped by that transport in two places -- the common block starts at byte 3
+ * of a 0x31, and the packet is signed -- so the entry point above refuses
+ * anything else. The consequence was reported by the owner on 2026-09-09, in
+ * exactly these words: "con usb no toma los niveles de vibracion". Plugging the
+ * pad in did not weaken the setting or change it; it silently stopped applying,
+ * while the panel that wrote it went on saying it had.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS DIFFERENT AND WHAT IS NOT. The common block is at byte 1 rather than
+ * byte 3, and there is no CRC: a wired 0x02 is not signed, and the four bytes
+ * that would carry a signature over Bluetooth are ordinary report bytes here.
+ * Nothing else differs. The rewrite is the same static function, so the mode
+ * and the gain mean the same thing on both transports by construction rather
+ * than by two implementations agreeing.
+ *
+ * THE LENGTH IS CHECKED AGAINST WHAT THE REWRITE READS, NOT AGAINST A PAD.
+ * Both pads measured here declare a 0x02 that is far longer than the minimum --
+ * 48 bytes on a DualSense, 64 on an Edge -- so the bound never fires for them.
+ * It is a bound on this function and not a claim about hardware: a report of
+ * some length neither descriptor declares is left alone rather than read past
+ * its end, and the client's own bytes then go to the pad as they always did.
+ *
+ * THE DEFECT IS MEASURED. emulation-111440.log holds both transports in one
+ * session under one registry: bus_create_hid_device brings the pad up with
+ * bus_type 2 at t=696586.859 and dualsense_vibration_options prints "output
+ * reports will be rewritten" for it, then brings the same model up with
+ * bus_type 1 at t=696666.844 and prints nothing at all. One transport reads
+ * the values, the other silently does not.
+ *
+ * THE BENEFIT IS NOT, and that is said here rather than left for somebody to
+ * discover. That log carries 10,676 wired writes of report 0x02 and four other
+ * traces carry two each -- 10,684 in all -- and NOT ONE of them carries a
+ * non-zero motor byte. Byte 39, the haptic path's own bit, is 0x00 in every
+ * one; six set the legacy bit and ask for zero with it. So on everything
+ * captured so far this function is a NO-OP: with no motor to scale the gain
+ * leaves the packet alone, and with no haptic bit the mode rewrite finds
+ * nothing to move. What a wired title would ask of the motors if it asked at
+ * all is UNKNOWN. The first use of this belongs in a +hid trace, where
+ * dualsense_vibration_fixup's own line says what it changed. */
+unsigned int dualsense_usb_vibration_fixup(unsigned char *report, unsigned int len,
+                                           const struct dualsense_vibration *vib,
+                                           struct dualsense_vibration_change *change)
+{
+    if (len < DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_VIBRATION_MIN || len > DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_MAX) return 0;
+    if (report[0] != DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_ID) return 0;
+    if (vib->mode == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_MODE_CLIENT && vib->gain == 100) return 0;
+    if (!dualsense_vibration_rewrite(report + DUALSENSE_USB_COMMON, vib, change)) return 0;
     return len;
 }
 
